Define the service window before calculating availability (@engineering-service-history: 0, is #chapter, #hephaestus-core "Distinguish-first-failure-from-restored-service.lingua#r_006C65B9E2B5F143EC63962FE9") { r_00A91BB3D08F5899C309280754
A device can be present, powered and moving while failing to deliver its intended function. Begin from [Connect realization to verification and intended use](Connect-realization-to-verification-and-intended-use.lingua#r_0C593F4A7A204FABB3B5876653) and [Keep service evidence meaningful through change and recovery](Keep-service-evidence-meaningful-through-change-and-recovery.lingua#r_0593D3DFF3699AB8D9E3ACE10B): name the delivered function, configuration, conditions and evidence. This block studies continuing service rather than another acceptance inspection. Its invented example is two document-processing stations that need a common local support service. A station contributes one unit of usable capacity; the service is useful only if the requested output is correct. The numerical model assumes that correctness classification has already been established. It cannot establish it by counting powered stations.

Let c(t) be usable capacity and d(t) the requested rate, both in work units per hour. For the observation interval [a,b), define capability time as the integral of 1[c(t)≥1], positive-demand time as the integral of 1[d(t)>0], fully met demand time as the integral of 1[d(t)>0 and c(t)≥d(t)], requested work as the integral of d(t), and delivered work as the integral of min(c(t),d(t)). The last expression assumes demand cannot be queued for later completion; if a backlog matters, reuse [Investigate production schedules rework and inventory time](Investigate-production-schedules-rework-and-inventory-time.lingua#r_00A308F2C75A43925C580DC3C9) and model arrivals and departures instead. These are different numerators and denominators. Use the calendar interval for capability availability, positive-demand time for demand-time availability, and requested work for the delivered fraction. A zero denominator is undefined, not perfect performance.

An original twelve-hour record has four intervals:

| Hours | Capacity | Requested rate |
| --- | --- | --- |
| [0,4) | 2 | 1 |
| [4,6) | 0 | 2 |
| [6,9) | 1 | 2 |
| [9,12) | 2 | 0 |

Capability availability is 10/12 = 5/6. Demand-time availability is 4/9. Requested work is 14 units; delivered work is 7, giving 1/2. None can replace the others. In particular the quiet final interval improves calendar capability time without satisfying any demand. Half-open intervals assign a transition to one interval and avoid double counting; isolated endpoints do not affect an ordinary time integral.

**Work:** calculate every quantity on [3,10), with units. Separate planned shutdown, failure, diagnosis, waiting for parts, active repair and acceptance work when the log supports those distinctions. A planned stoppage may be excluded from a predeclared operating window, but changing the denominator after seeing it biases comparisons. Missing intervals are unknown: do not relabel them as up or down. Partition the observation window completely before integrating; overlapping event reports cannot simply be summed as downtime. The study reuses the existing clipped-window helper only after validating this partition.

**Continue:** [Distinguish first failure from restored service](Distinguish-first-failure-from-restored-service.lingua#r_006C65B9E2B5F143EC63962FE9).

